 "name": "Colour Harmony of Historic Streets in Shanghai",
 "description": "Method replication: participants see 15 360° street panoramas (unrolled into wide strips) from Shanghai's historic conservation areas and rate, on the paper's two questionnaire indicators, how harmonious the building colours are and how attractive the colours look overall (1–5 each). The images are not the paper's stimuli (24 Baidu Street View panoramas, 2020–2023, taken on streets of the Hengshan Road–Fuxing Road historic area and the Bund area, chosen by Baidu street name; the bottom is cut off to hide the capture car).\nOriginal study: Yang, Deng, Shi, Wang, He, Xu & Xiao (2024), \"A novel approach for assessing color harmony of historical buildings via street view image\", Frontiers of Architectural Research 13, 764–775. Baidu 360° panoramas were taken on the roads next to Shanghai's 1,058 listed historic buildings; after colour correction and FCN segmentation, a colour-harmony model (analogous and complementary harmony with a hue tolerance) classified every building. A questionnaire of 30 residents and architecture professionals who had lived in Shanghai rated colour harmony and overall colour aesthetics on five levels, and its results correlated with the model (Spearman 0.746).\nDifferences from the original study: (1) the paper does not print the questionnaire wording or the five answer labels, so the questions and the 1–5 anchors here are written for this template; (2) images are sampled on streets of two historic areas, not at each listed building; (3) participants say whether they live in Shanghai or work in architecture instead of being recruited from those groups; (4) gender is asked.",
